{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc6dVkj9eMNRmqkShipQ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Flags in this project are booleans in `flags.toml`, and you read them through `flags.is_on()`. The code matches the stored convention.\n\n**How it works**\n- `flags.toml` has a `[flags]` table that maps each flag name to a bool. Right now it only has `invoice-pdf-v2 = false`.\n- `src/flags.py` loads that file once, when the module is imported. `is_on(name)` returns `True` only if the flag is set to true. A flag that isn't declared is treated as off, with no error.\n- `all_flags()` returns a copy of the whole table.\n\n**Adding the checkout flag**\n1. Declare it in `flags.toml`, with a kebab-case name, defaulting to off:\n   ```toml\n   [flags]\n   invoice-pdf-v2 = false\n   new-checkout-flow = false\n   ```\n2. Read it only through `is_on`:\n   ```python\n   from src.flags import is_on\n\n   if is_on(\"new-checkout-flow\"):\n       ...  # new flow\n   else:\n       ...  # existing flow\n   ```\n3. Turn it on by changing the value in `flags.toml`.\n\n**Things to know**\n- Flags are static. The file is read at import time, so changing a flag means editing the file and restarting. There is no per-user, per-environment or percentage rollout.\n- A typo in a flag name fails silently as \"off\", and the code doesn't enforce the \"must be declared\" rule. Double-check the spelling in both places.\n- Nothing calls `is_on` yet, so there's no existing call site to copy. `invoice-pdf-v2` is declared but unused.\n\nI can add the flag and wrap the checkout entry point if you tell me where that is. I haven't looked for it yet."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 939, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24572, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 939}, "output_